{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":60000,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-luna","packSha":"f83b8b0264b4eadb72acb596cd5208253113012ce1430e9d844732ecc3f9a71c","packTokens":14795,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":1,"sections":[{"id":"tpl","tokens":1353,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"self-contained","tokens":293,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl-end","tokens":49,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"test-kit","tokens":2337,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"approved-screens-rules","tokens":217,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"contract","tokens":117,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"acs","tokens":4232,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"approved-screens","tokens":2835,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"stubs","tokens":872,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"previous-tests","tokens":2008,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"pointers","tokens":32,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"failures","tokens":20,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":299,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"recap","tokens":35,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"author-tests"},"pointers":[{"path":"contracts/openapi.yaml","reason":"the locked API contract; the client and test handlers in lib/api are generated from it"}],"system":"You write black-box acceptance tests for a TypeScript web app (React, with the factory's component kit), one test per acceptance criterion, before the feature exists.\nRules:\n- Put the tests in tests/ at the repo root, as *.test.ts files (*.test.tsx for a screen test), with vitest (import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\"). The \"@/\" import alias points at the app's source root.\n- Test through public surfaces only: exported functions and modules, route handlers, and a screen as a person uses it.\n- Name each acceptance test exactly AC_<req>_<n>_<Words> (the it() title) for acceptance criterion AC-<req>.<n>, e.g. it(\"AC_1_2_ReturnsNotFoundWhenOrderMissing\", ...). Name characterisation tests CHAR_<Words>. The factory finds tests by these titles.\n- New APIs exist as stubs that throw new Error(\"not implemented\"); tests must import them and fail for now.\n- Also write characterisation tests for existing behaviour next to the change that must NOT change; those must pass today. A new app has little of its own: write none when there is nothing to keep.\n- Don't change production code, package.json, tsconfig or the vitest config.\n- Every test runs at a fixed instant in UTC, TEST_NOW (exported by tests/support/screen.ts): work out expected dates, ages and durations from TEST_NOW, never from the real clock, and never hard-code a value that only holds on one day.\n- You may run \"npx tsc --noEmit\" to check the tests compile. When every test is written, run your own new test files once (npx vitest run <your files>) and read how each test fails: on an assertion or \"not implemented\", never on a crash in the set-up or while rendering. Fix a test that crashes, or that passes, before you return. Run nothing else, and at most twice: the factory runs and judges every test itself in its test lab.\n- Test each criterion at its level: \"unit\" criteria call the module directly; this web app has no API of its own: the API is a separate service with locked tests of its own, and here it is reached only through the generated client. An \"api\" or \"job\" criterion is tested as what this app does with the API's answer: answer the call with the contract's test handlers or a mocked client, then assert what the screen or the web code does. Never test a rule only the server can keep (what is stored, what survives a restart, seeded records, the order a status may change in, what bad data is answered with) by calling the generated client and expecting the rule to hold: nothing in this repo may implement it. Never test the generated client itself. Skip \"manual\" criteria: a person checks those.\n- Outside systems (an email or SMS provider, a payment service, another company's API, a file store) are never called by a test: the test lab has no network. Put a stand-in behind the seam the code reaches the system through: replace the system's module with vi.mock. The stand-in records every call it gets; assert from that record what was sent (to whom, with which values, how many times), and make the stand-in fail where a criterion is about the system failing. Use a stand-in the tests already have for that system before you write one. A stand-in is test code: keep it with the tests, where it is locked with them.\n- A \"ui\" criterion gets a screen test: a tests/*.test.tsx file whose FIRST line is \"// @vitest-environment jsdom\". Render the screen's container (components/screens/<s-id>/container.tsx, the page with its data and behaviour) with render() from \"@testing-library/react\", and act as a person does with userEvent from \"@testing-library/user-event\".\n- Assert what a person sees and can do: find things by role and name (screen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Save\" })), by label (getByLabelText) or by the words on the page, never by class name, test id or component internals. Use findBy... for what appears after data loads.\n- There is no router, no server and no network in a screen test. Navigation is recorded: import { nav, at } from \"./support/screen\" (from tests/), call at(\"/orders/7\", { tab: \"paid\" }) before render() to set the address, and assert where the page went with nav.went (e.g. expect(nav.went).toContain(\"/orders/7\")).\n- Stand in for the API with vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", ...) or by mocking the generated client module (vi.mock), and answer with data in the shape the contract gives. Give each test its own answers; undo them in afterEach (vi.unstubAllGlobals(), vi.restoreAllMocks()).\n- A screen test proves behaviour on the screen: what is shown for which data, what a click or a typed value does, which message appears. It cannot judge looks (colour, spacing, a width): don't assert styles or class names.\n- Don't edit tests/support/ or the vitest config; the factory owns them. Don't use snapshots.\n\nA TEST MUST PASS OR FAIL ON THE CODE ALONE, on every run and every machine:\n- Time: no value that depends on today's date or the current time (use the fixed test instant, or values the test itself set).\n- Stored data: no reliance on rows, files or state another test or an earlier run left behind; create what you assert on.\n- Order: assert an order only where the criterion names one; otherwise compare as sets.\n- Numbers: compare computed decimals (ratios, averages, money after division) with a tolerance or at the precision shown.\n- Randomness: no assertion on a generated id, code or random value; assert that it exists, or use the value the response returned.\n- Actions: after a click, save or delete, wait for its result (findBy, waitFor or the awaited response) before asserting on it.\n- Lookups: find a value inside its row, card or region, never page-wide when the same text can appear twice.\n- Data: when a criterion says each, all, every, a list or an order, give it at least three rows of your own that differ.\n\n\n- Give no HTTP probes: the endpoints are served by the API, not by this app. Return an empty list of probes.\nReturn the list of tests you wrote (acId, file, method name).\n\nThe approved-screens section lists the screens a person approved for these requirements: route, states, and the exact words on them (title, buttons, field labels, column headers, empty, error, success and validation messages, toasts; \"change\" for a design note). Where a criterion is about what the user sees or is told, take the expected values from there, word for word, and do not invent other wording. A criterion with no screen there is tested as before. For each test that opens one of the approved screens, also return that screen's id as \"screen\" (the screen the test renders, which can be another than the one its requirement is listed under): the test is then held at the task that builds that screen. Leave it out for a test that opens no screen.\n\nAPI CONTRACT (locked: contracts/openapi.yaml). The app talks to the API only through the generated client in lib/api/client.ts; never write fetch calls or response types by hand, and never edit lib/api or contracts/openapi.yaml. Tests that need the API start the generated handlers from lib/api/client.msw.ts with msw's setupServer (msw/node); they answer with the contract's examples, so assert on that data.","tools":[],"user":"What is already in this checkout for your tests, so you need not search for it:\n\ntests/support/screen.ts (as it is; import from it, don't copy or change it):\n// Written by ai-factory from ai-factory design 20261014-hackathon-problem-statement-fe-95ff v1 (d1976b9e1eb0), approved by hamza (via web) on 2026-10-14.\n// Every test runs at TEST_NOW in UTC (set up in ./setup): work out expected dates and durations from it, never from the real clock.\n// (the factory's environment check runs the tests once more at another instant, through FACTORY_TEST_NOW)\nexport const TEST_NOW = new Date(process.env.FACTORY_TEST_NOW ?? \"2026-01-15T12:00:00.000Z\");\n\n// What a screen test reads and sets about the address. In tests the app's navigation (lib/nav) is replaced by this:\n// nothing really navigates, and every place the page went to is kept in nav.went.\nexport const nav = { path: \"/\", params: {} as Record<string, string>, went: [] as string[] };\n\n/** Put the test at an address before rendering, e.g. at(\"/orders/7\", { tab: \"paid\" }). */\nexport function at(path: string, params: Record<string, string> = {}): void {\n  nav.path = path;\n  nav.params = params;\n}\n\nexport function resetNav(): void {\n  nav.path = \"/\";\n  nav.params = {};\n  nav.went = [];\n}\n\ntests/support/setup.ts (as it is; import from it, don't copy or change it):\n// Written by ai-factory from ai-factory design 20261014-hackathon-problem-statement-fe-95ff v1 (d1976b9e1eb0), approved by hamza (via web) on 2026-10-14.\n// Runs before every test file. A screen test (a *.test.tsx file whose first line is \"// @vitest-environment jsdom\") renders\n// a page with no router and no browser: navigation is recorded in ./screen, and the few browser parts jsdom lacks are\n// filled in. A plain *.test.ts file is not affected.\nimport { afterEach, beforeEach, vi } from \"vitest\";\nimport { resetNav, TEST_NOW } from \"./screen\";\n\n// The same instant and time zone on every run and machine. Only Date is faked, and it moves on with real time from TEST_NOW,\n// so waits, timers and debounces work as usual.\nprocess.env.TZ = \"UTC\";\nbeforeEach(() => { vi.useFakeTimers({ toFake: [\"Date\"], now: TEST_NOW, shouldAdvanceTime: true }); });\n\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/nav\", async () => {\n  const { createElement } = await import(\"react\");\n  const { nav } = await import(\"./screen\");\n  return {\n    useNav: () => ({ path: nav.path, go: (to: string) => { nav.went.push(to); }, param: (name: string) => nav.params[name] ?? null }),\n    Link: ({ to, onClick, ...props }: { to: string; onClick?: (e: unknown) => void } & Record<string, unknown>) =>\n      createElement(\"a\", { ...props, href: to, onClick: (e: { preventDefault(): void }) => { onClick?.(e); e.preventDefault(); nav.went.push(to); } }),\n  };\n});\n\n// A page that uses the router's package itself, not lib/nav, gets the same stand-in: outside the app it has no router.\nvi.mock(\"next/navigation\", async (original) => {\n  const real = await original<typeof import(\"next/navigation\")>();\n  const { nav } = await import(\"./screen\");\n  const go = (to: string) => { nav.went.push(to); };\n  return {\n    ...real,\n    useRouter: () => ({ push: go, replace: go, back: () => {}, forward: () => {}, refresh: () => {}, prefetch: () => {} }),\n    usePathname: () => nav.path,\n    useSearchParams: () => new URLSearchParams(nav.params),\n    useParams: () => nav.params,\n  };\n});\n\nvi.mock(\"next/link\", async () => {\n  const { createElement } = await import(\"react\");\n  const { nav } = await import(\"./screen\");\n  return {\n    default: ({ href, onClick, prefetch: _p, replace: _r, scroll: _s, ...props }: { href: unknown; onClick?: (e: unknown) => void } & Record<string, unknown>) =>\n      createElement(\"a\", { ...props, href: String(href), onClick: (e: { preventDefault(): void }) => { onClick?.(e); e.preventDefault(); nav.went.push(String(href)); } }),\n  };\n});\n\n// A page is tested without the app's frame, so render() puts around it what the frame gives every page: the tooltip\n// provider (a page with a tooltip cannot render without it) and the place toasts appear in.\nvi.mock(\"@testing-library/react\", async (original) => {\n  const real = await original<typeof import(\"@testing-library/react\")>();\n  const { createElement, Fragment } = await import(\"react\");\n  const { TooltipProvider } = await import(\"@/components/ui/tooltip\");\n  const { Toaster } = await import(\"@/components/ui/sonner\");\n  const wrapper = ({ children }: { children: unknown }) => createElement(TooltipProvider, null, createElement(Fragment, null, children as never, createElement(Toaster)));\n  return { ...real, render: (ui: never, options?: object) => real.render(ui, { wrapper: wrapper as never, ...options }) };\n});\n\nif (typeof window !== \"undefined\") {\n  const noop = () => undefined;\n  class Observer { observe = noop; unobserve = noop; disconnect = noop; takeRecords = () => []; }\n  const w = window as unknown as Record<string, unknown>;\n  w.ResizeObserver ??= Observer;\n  w.IntersectionObserver ??= Observer;\n  w.matchMedia ??= (query: string) => ({ matches: false, media: query, onchange: null, addEventListener: noop, removeEventListener: noop, addListener: noop, removeListener: noop, dispatchEvent: () => false });\n  const el = Element.prototype as unknown as Record<string, unknown>;\n  el.scrollIntoView ??= noop;\n  el.hasPointerCapture ??= () => false;\n  el.setPointerCapture ??= noop;\n  el.releasePointerCapture ??= noop;\n}\n\nafterEach(async () => {\n  vi.useRealTimers();\n  resetNav();\n  if (typeof document !== \"undefined\") (await import(\"@testing-library/react\")).cleanup();\n});\n\nEach screen's code is components/screens/<screen id in lower case>/container.tsx: s-1, s-2, s-3, s-4, s-5, s-6, s-7, s-8, s-9, s-10, s-11, s-12, s-13, s-14, s-15, s-16, s-17, s-18.\n\nlib/api/client.msw.ts exports these API test handlers and example answers, generated from the contract with one example each. Start from them, and where a criterion says each, all, a list or an order, answer with rows of your own instead (the generator's single example cannot prove it): getSignInResponseMock, getGetOverviewResponseMock, getListLotsResponseMock, getGetLotLifecycleResponseMock, getListInventoryResponseMock, getCreateInventoryReceiptResponseMock, getGetInventoryProjectionsResponseMock, getListRationsResponseMock, getCreateRationResponseMock, getOptimizeRationResponseMock, getSaveRationCandidateResponseMock, getAssignRationCandidateResponseMock, getListFeedingPlansResponseMock, getCreateFeedingPlanResponseMock, getListFeedingActivitiesResponseMock, getCreateFeedingActivityResponseMock, getUpdateFeedingActivityResponseMock, getCancelFeedingActivityResponseMock, getListWeightMeasurementsResponseMock, getCreateWeightMeasurementResponseMock, getCorrectWeightMeasurementResponseMock, getGetYardSummaryResponseMock, getGetFeedToGainReportResponseMock, getExportFeedToGainReportResponseMock, getListAccountsResponseMock, getCreateAccountResponseMock, getUpdateAccountResponseMock, getSynchronizeSourceRecordsResponseMock, getGetDemoMaterialsResponseMock, getStartDemoFlowResponseMock, getGetLotPerformanceResponseMock, getGetLotFinancialsResponseMock, getSignInMockHandler, getGetOverviewMockHandler, getListLotsMockHandler, getGetLotLifecycleMockHandler, getListInventoryMockHandler, getCreateInventoryReceiptMockHandler, getGetInventoryProjectionsMockHandler, getListRationsMockHandler, getCreateRationMockHandler, getOptimizeRationMockHandler, getSaveRationCandidateMockHandler, getAssignRationCandidateMockHandler, getListFeedingPlansMockHandler, getCreateFeedingPlanMockHandler, getListFeedingActivitiesMockHandler, getCreateFeedingActivityMockHandler, getUpdateFeedingActivityMockHandler, getCancelFeedingActivityMockHandler, getListWeightMeasurementsMockHandler, getCreateWeightMeasurementMockHandler, getCorrectWeightMeasurementMockHandler, getGetYardSummaryMockHandler, getGetFeedToGainReportMockHandler, getExportFeedToGainReportMockHandler, getListAccountsMockHandler, getCreateAccountMockHandler, getUpdateAccountMockHandler, getSynchronizeSourceRecordsMockHandler, getGetDemoMaterialsMockHandler, getStartDemoFlowMockHandler, getGetLotPerformanceMockHandler, getGetLotFinancialsMockHandler, getFeedlotOperationsAPIMock.\n\n<artifact id=\"acs\" kind=\"acceptance-criteria\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"given\": \"An administrator-managed account exists and the sign-in page is open\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-1.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows email and password fields, no self-service registration or SSO controls, and the signed-in user's role and available pages\",\n  \"when\": \"The user inspects the sign-in controls and submits the account credentials\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"given\": \"An Administrator is signed in\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-1.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows account controls with email, password, and Administrator and Operator role values, plus user, import, configuration, plan, feeding, weight, dashboard, and report controls\",\n  \"when\": \"The Administrator opens user management and the main navigation\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"given\": \"An Operator is signed in\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-1.3\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows plan, feeding, weight, dashboard, and report controls and does not show user-management, import, or configuration controls\",\n  \"when\": \"The Operator opens the main navigation and user-management page\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-2\",\n  \"given\": \"The mock source contains PO and receiving records with vendor, cost, head count, weight, breed, age, and source fields\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-2.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The request targets the configured mock source and the screen shows the returned record fields and synchronization status for each record\",\n  \"when\": \"An Administrator starts synchronization\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-2\",\n  \"given\": \"A source record has a previously imported source record ID and another source record has failed synchronization\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-2.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the returned update outcome for the previously imported ID and the failed record's ID and error, without showing the failed record as a successful import\",\n  \"when\": \"An Administrator starts another synchronization\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-3\",\n  \"given\": \"The API provides a PO line mapped to multiple lots and pens, with available procurement and animal fields\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-3.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the PO line, source identifiers, related lots and pens, available vendor, cost, head-count, weight, breed, age, and source fields, and each pen's capacity and current animal count\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens the imported record details or procurement traceability view\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-3\",\n  \"given\": \"A PO line has a total cost of 1000 USD and two lots assigned 60 and 40 received animals\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-3.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows purchase-cost allocation values of 600 USD and 400 USD for the respective lots\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens the PO line's lot allocations\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-3\",\n  \"given\": \"The source response omits an animal's sex\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-3.3\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the sex field as Unknown\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens that animal's imported details\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-4\",\n  \"given\": \"The API provides a lot target weight, receiving date, and measured ADG\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-4.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the target weight, lifecycle milestones, measured ADG as the projection assumption, and projected market date\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens that lot's lifecycle view\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-4\",\n  \"given\": \"The API provides no usable measured ADG and provides the yard expected daily gain and any lot-specific target override\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-4.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the projected market date, target weight used, and configured yard expected daily gain as the projection assumption\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens that lot's lifecycle view\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-5\",\n  \"given\": \"The API provides an inventory item with stock values, entered units, cost, nutrition fields, and storage location\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-5.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the item name, category, available quantity, entered unit, cost, dry-matter percentage, energy value, storage location, and opening, received, consumed, and closing stock values\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens the feed inventory item\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-5\",\n  \"given\": \"An inventory item is entered in tons with a cost per ton\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-5.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the entered units and normalized calculation values in pounds and USD per pound\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens its cost details\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-6\",\n  \"given\": \"Inventory, projected consumption, and a configured wastage ratio are available\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-6.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows estimates for daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, six-month, and annual horizons and the wastage ratio used\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens inventory projections\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-6\",\n  \"given\": \"The inventory movement response contains receipts and feed consumption\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-6.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the returned stock movements and no separate wastage movement\",\n  \"when\": \"A user reviews the movement list and forecast\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-7\",\n  \"given\": \"A user enters a ration name, type, ingredients, proportions, and intended animal type or weight range\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-7.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the returned ration's ingredients, proportions, dry-matter percentage, energy value, intended range, and calculated cost per pound in USD\",\n  \"when\": \"The user saves and opens the ration\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-7\",\n  \"given\": \"A ration's ingredients have costs entered in different supported weight units\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-7.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the calculated cost in USD per pound and retains the entered units for display\",\n  \"when\": \"The user opens the ration cost details\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-8\",\n  \"given\": \"A user has entered nutrition or animal-range constraints and candidate mixes are returned\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-8.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows each candidate's ingredient proportions, cost, and trade-offs without marking it saved or assigned\",\n  \"when\": \"The user requests and views candidate mixes\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-8\",\n  \"given\": \"An optimization candidate is displayed\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-8.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the returned candidate as saved or assigned only after the action is accepted\",\n  \"when\": \"The user selects save or assign and that action is accepted\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-9\",\n  \"given\": \"A pen plan uses a bunk rate of 25 pounds per animal per